      "claim": "L5 bidirectional transport for lorcaserin and loratadine shows high Papp with efflux ratios ~1.0-1.2, indicating no significant gut-wall efflux for similar lipophilic basic chloroarenes (C137,C150,C164,C179,C156,C133,C138,C131); domperidone and linezolid also show high permeability (C153,C146). Efflux examples such as GV196771, AFN-1252, and compound 1a are not query-related and do not constitute affirmative low-F evidence (C140,C152,C158,C136).",
      "card_ids": ["C137","C150","C164","C179","C156","C133","C138","C131","C153","C146","C140","C152","C158","C136"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic clearance/metabolic stability evidence is mixed: lorcaserin, a similar lipophilic basic chloroarene, has low HLM CLint 18.57 µL/min/mg and is metabolized by multiple CYPs/FMO (C209,C180,C227); loratadine has HLM CLint 137 but human hepatocyte CLint 9.5-26.4 µL/min/10^6 cells and is orally bioavailable (C211,C205); prenylated flavonoids show moderate CYP metabolism (C224,C213,C225,C195,C223). PF-06282999, a thiouracil, has short rat liver microsomal half-life (C198,C183,C228) and FMO1 CLint 97.7 (C203), but this is a different thiouracil scaffold in rat microsomes and is outweighed by human F of propylthiouracil (C02) and high permeability of cyclic thiourea analogs. L6 does not provide affirmative threshold-relevant evidence of F < 20%.",
